---
/**
 * The lineage argument, in the order it has to be made: what is inherited,
 * where each neighboring field stops, the one question those stops leave
 * open, why it is urgent now, the theory's names for the mechanism, and what
 * the established fields say back.
 *
 * Rendered on the About page and in the essay "What Ethotechnics is not"
 * from one source (content/lineage.ts), so the two cannot drift. No
 * headings inside: the host section supplies them at its own level.
 */
import {
  disputes,
  inheritedTraditions,
  neighboringFields,
  theoryVocabulary,
  whyNow,
} from "../content/lineage";
---

<div class="lineage stack-sm">
  <p>
    Ethotechnics does not claim originality for the proposition that human
    struggle should be reduced, or that institutions exploit unpriced labor to
    absorb their own failures. Those insights belong to established traditions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      inheritedTraditions.map((tradition) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{tradition.name}:</strong> {tradition.finding}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of these moral propositions is individually original to Ethotechnics.
    Nor is the mechanism at its center. The fields closest to it each see part
    of it and stop at a different point:
  </p>
  <div class="mapping-table__wrapper">
    <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--stack lineage__table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Field</th>
          <th scope="col">What it sees</th>
          <th scope="col">Where it stops</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          neighboringFields.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" data-label="Field">
                {row.field}
                <span class="lineage__works small muted">
                  {row.works.map((work, index) => (
                    <>
                      {work.authors && `${work.authors}, `}
                      {work.form === "book" ? (
                        <cite>{work.title}</cite>
                      ) : work.form === "article" ? (
                        <>"{work.title}"</>
                      ) : (
                        work.title
                      )}{" "}
                      ({work.year}){index < row.works.length - 1 && ";"}
                    </>
                  ))}
                </span>
              </th>
              <td data-label="What it sees">{row.sees}</td>
              <td data-label="Where it stops">
                {row.stops}{" "}
                <a class="lineage__essay" href={row.essay.href}>
                  {row.essay.title}&nbsp;→
                </a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Read together, the stops trace one gap. Safety science follows the lost
    signal, but inside the organization and after a catastrophe. Due process
    follows the person, but only as far as their own case. Labor and burden
    studies count the cost, but not the signal the cost hides. Risk frameworks
    monitor, but leave the monitoring to the operator. None asks what an
    institution owes when the person who absorbed the error is outside it, the
    harm is routine rather than catastrophic, and the remedy has to reach the
    rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ethotechnics starts from that question. Its claim: institutions absorb the
    evidence that their automated systems are wrong, and the absorption keeps
    the error from anyone who could change the rule. The essay on
    <a href="/research/theory/insulation">insulation</a> states it in full.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Why the question is urgent now:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    {
      whyNow.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{item.label}.</strong> {item.text}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>The theory's names for the mechanism:</p>
  <ul>
    {
      theoryVocabulary.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{item.term}:</strong> {item.text}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    The claims meet established fields, and the fields push back. Each claim's
    strongest counter, stated in its own terms, the answer, and the measurement
    that would settle it:
  </p>
  <div class="mapping-table__wrapper">
    <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--stack lineage__table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Claim</th>
          <th scope="col">Strongest counter</th>
          <th scope="col">The answer</th>
          <th scope="col">What would settle it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          disputes.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" data-label="Claim">
                {row.claim}
                <span class="lineage__works small muted">
                  {row.counterSource}
                </span>
              </th>
              <td data-label="Strongest counter">{row.counter}</td>
              <td data-label="The answer">{row.answer}</td>
              <td data-label="What would settle it">
                <a href={row.instrumentHref}>{row.instrument}</a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>
